 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>MAFF supplies the cleaning/tenderizing-before-seasoning reference.</li><li>The separate browning, reduced glaze and folding into plain rice are this site’s revised home method.</li><li>All octopus is cooked outside the rice cooker.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Adaptation and sources"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Pink colour alone does not mean tender.</li><li>Cool a test piece and bite it: it should yield easily.</li><li>Continue gentle cooking if chewy; a brief sear will not tenderize it.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Tenderness controls the first stage"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Discard the octopus cooking liquor.</li><li>Fold in only drained, thinly coated pieces, not the leftover pan sauce.</li><li>Divide the finished mixture into six equal portions rather than assuming a fixed <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"90\" data-s=\"0\">90 g</span> ball.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Keep the rice dry and fluffy"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Eat freshly made.</li><li>For later, cool rice quickly and refrigerate ideally within one hour; use within 24 hours.</li><li>Add nori just before eating.</li><li>Texture will firm in the fridge, but that is not a reason to leave rice out.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Serve or chill promptly"
  }
 ],
